All the wonderful and unprecedented phenomena became manifest. The great hermit showed these omens to his disciples, and having increased their devotion towards the Fully Enlightened Buddhas even more, he passed away and was reborn in the Brahma world. While those disciples were performing the funeral rites for his body, he appeared in a visible form and said, "I am your teacher, reborn in the Brahma world. Do not be negligent; strive in the worship of the sand stupa and be devoted to the practice of meditation." Having said this, he returned to the Brahma world.

Thus, having wandered among devas and humans, he was reborn in a householder family in Sāvatthī during the time of this Buddha. From the day he was born, as the glory and wealth of that family increased, they named him "Sirimā." When he was old enough to walk, a younger brother was born; saying, "This one is born increasing the glory," they named him "Sirivaḍḍha." Both brothers, upon seeing the Buddha's power during the acceptance of the Jetavana monastery, gained faith and ordained as monks. Among them, Sirivaḍḍha had not yet attained the superhuman states (uttarimanussadhamma), but he was a recipient of the four requisites and was honored and respected by both laypeople and monks. The Elder Sirimā, however, from the time of his ordination, was one who received few gains due to a certain past kamma that left a flaw, and he was not highly regarded by the many. Nevertheless, while practicing tranquillity and insight (samatha-vipassanā), he soon became an Arahant endowed with the six supernormal powers (chaḷabhiññā). This matter is stated in the Apadāna (Ap. ii, 49, 111-147) as follows:

"In the Himavanta mountains, there was a hermit named Devala;
There, I had a walking path created by non-human beings.

Always bearing the weight of matted hair and carrying a water-pot,
Seeking the supreme goal, I then emerged from the forest.

Eighty-four thousand disciples used to attend to me;
They lived in the forest then, devoted to their own tasks.

Having emerged from the hermitage, I made a sand stupa;
Having brought various flowers, I worshipped that stupa.

Having gained confidence of mind in that stupa, I entered the hermitage;
All the disciples, having assembled, asked me about this matter."
